<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Seychelles</h1><p class="st-meta">1976 CE – present · capital Victoria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An archipelago of 115 islands in the Indian Ocean, independent from Britain in 1976, a one-party state until 1991 and now a multi-party republic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-seychelles">British Seychelles</a> gained independence from the United Kingdom in 1976 as a republic within the Commonwealth, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-mancham">James Mancham</a> as first president. In 1977 a coup d'état by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france-albert-rene">France-Albert René</a> ousted him. The state has had a president as head of state and government ever since.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>René discouraged over-dependence on tourism and wanted &quot;to keep Seychelles for the Seychellois&quot;. The 1979 constitution declared a socialist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, which lasted until 1991. There were several coup attempts against René in the 1980s, some supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. In 1981 Mike Hoare led 43 South African mercenaries in a failed attempt, and in 1986 an attempted coup led by the Minister of Defence, Ogilvy Berlouis, prompted René to ask <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for help. The first draft of a new constitution failed to win the required 60% in 1992, and an amended version was approved in 1993. The economy grew from agriculture to services, the public sector and tourism.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>From the 1977 coup until the October 2020 general election the president always came from the same party. Wavel Ramkalawan, the first president outside it, won in 2020. In October 2025 Patrick Herminie of United Seychelles won a presidential runoff and was sworn in on 26 October 2025 as the sixth president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/seychelles.jpg" alt="Seychelles" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Vxb83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Seychelles.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1976 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Victoria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>James Mancham, France-Albert René, Wavel Ramkalawan, Patrick Herminie</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (61.3% Catholic in the 2022 census)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, French, Seychellois Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/seychelles">Seychelles</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Seychelles</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2001&amp;focus=seychelles" aria-label="Seychelles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6218 2351 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Seychelles lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6538 2607L6538 2607"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6538 2607h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6538 2607h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6538 2607h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Victoria. <a href="/atlas?year=2001&amp;focus=seychelles">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
